# RMBS Natural Language Query

Ask questions of loan-level RMBS data in ordinary language, and get answers you can verify. A language model converts each question into a structured query spec, and a compiler turns the spec into SQL using metric definitions held in code, so the model never does the arithmetic. Each answer comes with its spec, its Oracle SQL and its result table, and any number in the answer text that isn't in the table is rejected.

All data here is synthetic, covering {{n_deals}} Australian RMBS deals, {{n_loans}} loans and {{n_loan_months}} loan-months from 2021-10 to 2026-09, in 2 trustee formats with the inconsistencies real trustee data has. The reasoning behind the design is in [docs/DESIGN_RESPONSE.md](docs/DESIGN_RESPONSE.md).

## Results

{{#eval}}
**Compared with direct SQL, on 40 questions.** On the {{answer_n}} questions with a numeric answer, a model writing SQL directly got {{base_answer_k}} right and the compiled approach {{sys_answer_k}}. Neither returned an incorrect number ({{base_wrong_numbers}} and {{sys_wrong_numbers}}), so every miss on both sides was a refusal to answer.

- The compiled approach didn't recognise "Queensland", and asked for a day threshold on questions phrased in missed payments.
- Direct SQL declined all {{base_ambig_declined}} ambiguous questions, where the compiled approach asked what was meant.

Overall, the compiled approach got {{sys_total_k}} of {{sys_total_n}} ({{sys_total_pct}}, 95% interval {{sys_total_lo}} to {{sys_total_hi}}) and direct SQL {{base_total_k}} of {{base_total_n}} ({{base_total_pct}}, interval {{base_total_lo}} to {{base_total_hi}}). The direct SQL prompt contained the full metric definitions and the exact output columns, so this compares 2 ways of applying the same definitions, not definitions against none. The design response discusses what that means.

